{% import "tty/core.njk" as tty %}
{#
  The email row spells the separators out in words. There is no address on this
  page, in any encoding, and no `mailto:` link.

  This replaced an HTML-numeric-character-reference version of the address. That
  version was weak for the reason the owner gave: an encoding is a reversible
  map, so `html.unescape` — one line — turns it straight back into a harvestable
  address. Words are not a map. A scraper has to guess that `[at]` means `@` and
  `[dot]` means `.`, and guess it for every variant every site invents.

  So the rule here is: never put the address on this page in ANY reversible
  form. That rules out numeric entities, hex entities, a reversed string with
  `unicode-bidi`, a `mailto:` with percent-escapes, and a data URI. Each of
  those is one transform away from plain text.

  What this costs: the address is not clickable, and a reader has to retype it.
  That is the trade the owner chose. To give the click back, the address has to
  be assembled in the browser at click time, which means a fourth script — and
  `check-build.sh` asserts the script count, so that is a deliberate change, not
  a quiet one.

  A screen reader reads this literally: "elliancarlos bracket at bracket gmail
  bracket dot bracket com". That is verbose but unambiguous. Do NOT add an
  `aria-label` with the plain address to tidy it up — that puts the address back
  in the markup and undoes the whole point.

  `data-contact-email` is the assertion's hook, not a styling one. It carries no
  CSS. `check-build.sh` reads the element's text and asserts it does NOT parse as
  an address, so the separators can be rewritten without turning CI red.
#}
<div class="tty-screen">
  <section>
    <h1 class="tty-h1">{{ i18n.en.contact.title }}</h1>
    <div class="tty-linklist" style="margin-top: 22px">
      <div class="tty-linklist__row">
        <span class="tty-linklist__key">{{ i18n.en.contact.email }}</span>
        <span data-contact-email>elliancarlos [at] gmail [dot] com</span>
      </div>
      <div class="tty-linklist__row">
        {# "github", not "git" — the row links a forge account, and git is a
           version control system, not an account. Same key on the homepage. #}
        <span class="tty-linklist__key">github</span>
        <a href="{{ seo.social.github }}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">github.com/elliancarlos</a>
      </div>
      <div class="tty-linklist__row">
        <span class="tty-linklist__key">work</span>
        <a href="{{ seo.social.linkedin }}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">linkedin.com/in/elliancarlos</a>
      </div>
      {# The Brazilian research curriculum, hosted on Lattes. The site calls this
         "academic cv" and nothing else: the label is i18n[locale].nav.academicCv,
         the same string the footer and the homepage use. It was briefly labelled
         "lattes" here, which gave one URL two names on one site.

         The URL itself comes from seo.social.lattes, so no template holds a second
         copy of it. That constant is https; CNPq also answers on http, and there is
         no reason to ship the weaker scheme.

         The visible text is the URL, following every other row on this page. Only
         the key column carries the name. #}
      <div class="tty-linklist__row">
        <span class="tty-linklist__key">{{ i18n.en.nav.academicCv }}</span>
        <a href="{{ seo.social.lattes }}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">lattes.cnpq.br/3423853440338718</a>
      </div>
      <div class="tty-linklist__row">
        <span class="tty-linklist__key">orcid</span>
        <a href="{{ seo.social.orcid }}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">orcid.org/0009-0004-9135-8207</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
